# Repair plan for /go-test/go-test:fixture.buildcodefix.tests.suite::TestAdd

## Diagnosis

`services/calc/calc.go` is the entire production surface of the `calc` service:

```
package calc

func Add(a, b int) int { return a - b }
```

The exported `Add` is named and consumed as integer addition, but its body
performs subtraction. The suite calls `calc.Add(2, 3)` and expects `5`; the
current body evaluates `2 - 3 = -1`, which is exactly the observed failure
(`calc_test.go:10: Add(2, 3) = -1, want 5`). This is a single sign/operator
defect in production code, not a test or harness problem.

Supporting facts gathered:

- `docs/architecture/architecture.yaml` declares one service, `calc`, at
  `services/calc`, language `go`, and one suite command for record/replay/live:
  `go test -json -count=1 ./tests/suite/...`.
- `go.mod` declares module `fixture/buildcodefix`, so the test's import
  `fixture/buildcodefix/services/calc` resolves to `services/calc/calc.go`.
  There is no second implementation, build tag, generated file, or alternate
  package shadowing it — `services/calc/calc.go` and `tests/suite/calc_test.go`
  are the only `.go` files in the tree.
- Nothing else in the repository calls `Add`, so correcting the operator has no
  other caller to regress.

## Contract to implement

`calc.Add(a, b int) int` must return the integer sum of its two arguments:
for all `a, b`, `Add(a, b) == a + b`. In particular it must be commutative in
the arguments (`Add(2, 3) == Add(3, 2) == 5`), return `a` when `b == 0`, and
return `0` when both arguments are `0`. Keep the existing exported signature,
name, parameter order, and package (`package calc`) unchanged — the suite
imports and calls it exactly as written today, so any signature change would
break compilation.

